<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Amazing Community.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Determine the gist of simple stories or reports related to familiar subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can determine the gist of simple stories about community traditions and events (e.g., 'In our community, we celebrate La Mejorana Festival with music and dancing.' 'The festival takes place in the town square every September.').
                Can identify details about familiar stories about the community and its people (e.g., 'One landmark of the community is the old church.').
                Can recognize main points in group discussions about the community (e.g., 'We have planned a community celebration for this weekend.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of a vibrant community showing scenes like a park, a school, a market, and a festival. The teacher points to each picture and asks, "What do you see here? Is this a park? Is this a school?" Students respond with simple answers. The teacher introduces the theme "This Is My Amazing Community."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words using flashcards with clear images (e.g., `community`, `park`, `school`, `festival`, `market`, `teacher`, `farmer`, `cook`, `celebrate`, `delicious`). For each word, the teacher models clear pronunciation, repeating it twice. Emphasis is placed on stress in `neighborhood` (primary stress on 'neigh-') and on short and long vowel sounds for `cook` (/ʊ/) and `school` (/uː/). The teacher uses gestures or actions for verbs like `cook` and `celebrate`. Example: "This is a `community`." (points to a diverse group of people). "This is a `park`." (points to a picture of a park).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary using simple questions and examples.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `park` a place where you play outside?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we `cook` food or books?" (Expected answer: "Food.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `festival` a big, fun `event`?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it is!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you buy fresh food at the `market`?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story (narrative excerpt, approx. 60-90 seconds) about "Our Community Day". The audio describes simple activities and roles using the new vocabulary (community, park, school, festival, market, teacher, farmer, cook, celebrate, delicious). The narrator speaks clearly and at a slow pace appropriate for A2.1. Example sentences: "In our `community`, we have a big `festival` every year. It's a `delicious` `event`! People `gather` in the `park` and `celebrate`. Our `farmer`s bring food to the `market`. The `teacher`s from the `school` help too."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can understand short, simple narratives or descriptions about familiar topics when spoken slowly and clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What is the story mainly about? A: animals, B: a community festival, C: space?" (Students choose B). "What happens in the community every year?" (Expected: a festival).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Details):</b> Students listen again. The teacher then presents True/False statements related to the audio, or shows pictures and asks students to point to what they hear. Example: "The festival is in the school. (True/False)." "The farmers bring delicious food. (True/False)." "People cook books at the market. (True/False)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Check:</b> The teacher asks students to raise their hand when they hear one of the new vocabulary words.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening for specific information and target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details and identifying target vocabulary (`community`, `park`, `school`, `festival`, `market`, `teacher`, `farmer`, `cook`, `celebrate`, `delicious`). Grammar focus: Present simple questions related to community activities (e.g., 'What delicious food do people cook at the market?'). Pronunciation: Short /ʊ/ in 'cook', long /uː/ in 'school', stress in compound nouns like `neighborhood` (first syllable).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listen and Complete:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the audio story, but with key vocabulary words missing. The teacher plays short snippets of the audio, pausing after each sentence. Students listen carefully and write the missing word. Example: "The ___ gathers at the ___ before the festival every year." (community, park). Teacher repeats as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Listen and Identify:</b> The teacher plays the audio again, focusing on sentences with target pronunciation points. Students listen and repeat, paying attention to the teacher's modeling of /ʊ/ in 'cook' and /uː/ in 'school'. The teacher provides minimal pairs or similar-sounding words if appropriate for /ʊ/ and /uː/ distinction. The teacher then asks students to identify the stressed syllable in words like `neighborhood` or `market` as they hear them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Listen and Ask:</b> In pairs, students receive a short list of simple questions about the community audio (e.g., "What does the farmer do?", "Where do people `celebrate`?", "Do they `cook` `delicious` food?"). The teacher plays the audio one more time. Students listen for the answers. After the audio, one student asks a question, and their partner tries to answer based on what they heard. They check their answers against each other. Teacher circulates and provides support for forming simple present simple questions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Listening and Reporting (Small Group Discussion).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher introduces a new short audio (approx. 45-60 seconds) about another aspect of the community, for example, a short interview with "Mrs. Sofia, the local `teacher`" or "Mr. Ricardo, the `gardener` from the `park`," detailing a typical day or a specific `event`. The audio still uses the target vocabulary and A2.1 level grammar (present simple, simple questions). Students work in small groups of 3-4. They listen to the audio twice. Their task is to discuss and agree on:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the main `job` of the person speaking? (e.g., "Mrs. Sofia is a `teacher` at the `school`.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one activity they mention? (e.g., "She likes to `share` stories.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What do they say about the `community`? (e.g., "Our `community` is very `friendly`.")</li>
                </ul>
                After listening, each group `discusses` their findings and prepares to share one main point with the class. This encourages them to `share` community activities and `discuss` community roles (`What does your neighbor do?`).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategies and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group discussion and reporting in Stage 4. The teacher notes if students are actively listening, using target vocabulary, and determining the gist of the story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally reports one main idea or fact they identified from the listening task in Stage 4. For instance, "We heard that Mrs. Sofia is a `teacher`, and she helps children at the `school`." Or, "The `gardener` `builds` new flower beds in the `park`."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target vocabulary (e.g., `community`, `teacher`, `park`) clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students attempt to use at least 2-3 of the introduced vocabulary words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> The reported gist is clear and understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students demonstrate participation in the group discussion.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with two prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write one new word you learned today about our `community`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one interesting thing you heard about a person or place in the `community`?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I understand the different stories about the `community` today? (Easy, Okay, Difficult)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What helped me understand the audio messages better?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students determine the gist of the simple stories? (e.g., visual aids, repetitive listening, clear pronunciation modeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next listening lesson? (e.g., more pre-teaching of specific phrases, simpler audio, more guided note-taking).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully identify the core vocabulary? Was the pronunciation practice effective?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we listened to stories about our amazing community and learned new words. Next lesson, we will *read* a short text about a community event, using these same words, to practice identifying important details and understanding information in written form."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>